[gd_scene load_steps=8 format=3]

; REACH-1 (2026-09-19) — AN OVERTURNED BIN, as a movable prop with a HOLLOW collider.
;
; §5b's fourth planted case is "under an overturned bin (allowed)": the object is covered by
; something the seeker can pick up and throw aside, which is legal and is the game. Proving that
; needs a prop the eye ray HITS and the crate underneath does NOT overlap — so a single box
; collider is wrong twice over (it would swallow the crate, and the rest audit would then
; correctly report a prop overlap that the fixture never meant to create).
;
; So: five box shapes — a lid and four sides, 0.08 m thick, open at the bottom. Outer 1.0 x 0.70 x
; 1.0, interior 0.84 x 0.62 x 0.84, which clears a 0.44 m crate by 0.20 m on every side.
;
; THE FIRST CollisionShape3D CHILD IS THE LID, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. PlacementIntegrity measures
; a prop by its first collider (CARRY-1's choice: the props in this game are single-shape boxes and
; spheres, and walking every shape would cost a query per shape on a path that runs once per settle
; for every prop in the room). A multi-shape fixture is therefore audited by its lid alone. That is
; fine HERE — the bin is scenery for the case, never its subject — and it is written down because
; the day a real multi-shape prop ships, this is the line that has to change.
;
; Named "CollisionShape3D" for the same mundane reason Crate.tscn's is: PropManager.AuthoredKindOf
; looks that name up to infer PropKind from the shape, and a box means Crate.

